# TTPXHunter Reproduction

Production-style Python reproduction of the original `TTPXHunter.ipynb` workflow. The package extracts MITRE ATT&CK technique IDs from cyber threat reports with the Hugging Face model `nanda-rani/TTPXHunter`, preserves the SharpPanda notebook baseline, and includes a CISA TTP Articles benchmark workflow.

The original notebook used `list(set(...))`, so output ordering was not stable. This version defaults to deterministic first-seen ordering while keeping a `legacy-set` mode for closer notebook behavior.

## Repository Layout

```text
.
├── src/ttpxhunter/       # Python package and unified CLI
├── model_artifacts/            # label_dict.pkl and ttp_id_name.pkl
├── examples/reports/           # sample SharpPanda report
├── datasets/cisa/              # CISA TTP Articles dataset files
├── datasets/mitre_attack/      # copied MITRE ATT&CK training CSV
├── results/                    # generated reproduction and benchmark outputs
├── tests/                      # offline and opt-in integration tests
├── pyproject.toml              # package metadata, pytest config, console script
├── requirements.txt            # dependency ranges
└── requirements-lock.txt       # pinned environment used for this reproduction
```

## Setup

Create an isolated environment and install the package in editable mode:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel
python -m pip install -e ".[test]"
```

To recreate the pinned dependency set first:

```bash
python -m pip install -r requirements-lock.txt
python -m pip install -e . --no-deps
```

If NumPy metadata or wheel resolution fails, recreate the environment and force
binary wheels:

```bash
rm -rf .venv
python3 -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip setuptools wheel
python -m pip install --only-binary=:all: -r requirements.txt
python -m pip install -e . --no-deps
```

## CLI Usage

After editable installation, use either the console script:

```bash
ttpxhunter --help
```

or the module entrypoint:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter --help
```

Run the SharpPanda sample report and compare it with the saved notebook baseline:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter infer \
  examples/reports/SharpPanda_APT_Campaign_Expands_its_Arsenal_Targeting_G20_Nations.txt \
  --compare tests/expected_sharppanda.json \
  --output results/sharppanda.json \
  --json
```

Use notebook-like set ordering when needed:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter infer \
  examples/reports/SharpPanda_APT_Campaign_Expands_its_Arsenal_Targeting_G20_Nations.txt \
  --order legacy-set
```

Useful inference options include `--threshold`, `--model-id`, `--revision`, `--device`, `--batch-size`, `--label-dict`, and `--ttpid2name`.

## CISA Benchmark Workflow

The CISA workflow targets Zenodo record `14659512` / DOI
`10.5281/zenodo.14659512`.

Sơ đồ workflow tổng quát của TTPXHunter, dựa trên paper và source reproduce
trong repo này, nằm ở
[`docs/TTPXHUNTER_WORKFLOW.md`](docs/TTPXHUNTER_WORKFLOW.md).

For the full explanation of how CISA advisories become benchmark inputs and
ground-truth labels, see
[`docs/CISA_BENCHMARK_WORKFLOW.md`](docs/CISA_BENCHMARK_WORKFLOW.md).

Download or refresh the dataset:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter cisa download --out-dir datasets/cisa
```

Describe the local dataset:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter cisa describe \
  datasets/cisa/CISA-crawl-rt-ttp-ct.json \
  --json
```

Run a quick three-article benchmark:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter cisa benchmark \
  datasets/cisa/CISA-crawl-rt-ttp-ct.json \
  --limit 3 \
  --output results/cisa/cisa_benchmark_limit3.json \
  --report-md results/cisa/cisa_benchmark_limit3_report.md \
  --rows-csv results/cisa/cisa_benchmark_limit3_rows.csv \
  --charts-dir results/cisa/charts_limit3
```

Run the full benchmark:

```bash
python -m ttpxhunter cisa benchmark \
  datasets/cisa/CISA-crawl-rt-ttp-ct.json \
  --device mps \
  --batch-size 32 \
  --output results/cisa/cisa_benchmark_full.json \
  --report-md results/cisa/cisa_benchmark_full_report.md \
  --rows-csv results/cisa/cisa_benchmark_full_rows.csv \
  --charts-dir results/cisa/charts_full
```

The benchmark normalizes CISA labels before scoring:

- tactics (`TA####`) are dropped;
- sub-techniques (`T####.###`) are collapsed to parent techniques;
- malformed ATT&CK-like IDs are ignored;
- by default, expected labels are filtered to the TTPXHunter model label space.

Use `--expected-mode all-base` to score against all normalized base techniques, including IDs the model cannot emit.

Current saved full-run baseline:

```text
articles=77
expected=1325
predicted=2112
tp=516
fp=1596
fn=809
micro_precision=0.244318
micro_recall=0.389434
micro_f1=0.300262
```

The benchmark report can include SVG charts for metrics, TP/FP/FN counts, top missing techniques, top extra predictions, and article-level F1 distribution. Those charts are generated without extra plotting dependencies.

## Testing

Fast tests avoid loading the Hugging Face model:

```bash
python -m pytest
```

Latest local run:

```text
18 passed, 1 skipped in 0.49s
```

The full SharpPanda reproduction test downloads and runs the Hugging Face model:

```bash
RUN_HF_INTEGRATION=1 python -m pytest tests/test_integration_sharppanda.py
```

## Reproducibility Notes

- `model_artifacts/label_dict.pkl` maps model labels to ATT&CK technique IDs.
- `model_artifacts/ttp_id_name.pkl` maps ATT&CK technique IDs to names.
- The default model revision is not pinned; pass `--revision <hf-commit>` when   an exact Hugging Face revision is required.
- The notebook threshold is preserved as `0.644`.
- NLTK `punkt` and `punkt_tab` data are downloaded automatically if missing.
- `datasets/mitre_attack/MITRE_ATT&CK_Dataset.csv` is kept for completeness; single-report inference only needs the report text, model artifacts, and Hugging Face model.

## Limitations

- Full inference requires downloading `nanda-rani/TTPXHunter` unless it is already cached locally.
- CISA benchmark quality depends on label normalization because the CISA source mixes tactics, base techniques, sub-techniques, and noisy regex matches.
- Saved benchmark results are reproduction artifacts, not a general claim about model performance on all threat intelligence reports.
